---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import { StatsMap } from "@/components/StatsMap";
---

<Layout
  title="PA Housing Stats — municipal permits, population, and burden"
  description="Pennsylvania municipal-level housing stats: building permits per capita, population change, median home value, and rent burden across all 2,500+ PA municipalities."
>
  <header class="page-header">
    <div class="page-header__inner">
      <p class="page-header__eyebrow">PA Housing Stats</p>
      <h1>
        Where housing is getting built, where it isn&rsquo;t, and where
        the pressure is highest
      </h1>
      <p class="page-header__lede">
        A municipal-level companion to the <a href="/">vote map</a>. The vote
        map shows how <em>legislators</em> have voted on housing bills. This
        page shows what&rsquo;s actually happening on the ground in their
        districts: how many permits each muni is issuing per capita, how
        much population each muni has gained or lost since 2020, and how
        burdened renters and owners are today.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main id="stats-map" class="page-main">
    <StatsMap client:only="react" />
  </main>

  <section class="page-notes">
    <h2>Reading this map</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Pick a metric</strong> at the top of the map. The choropleth
        re-colors all 2,500+ Pennsylvania municipalities to that metric.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Click any muni</strong> for the full per-muni stat sheet:
        population, permits, median home value, rent / owner burden, and
        (where available) population change since 2020.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>County lines</strong> toggle on the top-left helps orient
        you in less familiar regions.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>About the data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Permits per 1k residents / year (2020-2024)</strong> &mdash; Census
        Building Permits Survey (BPS) annual files, plus parcel-level
        OpenDataPhilly + WPRDC permits for Philadelphia and Pittsburgh. The
        muni-level rate shown here is the city-wide aggregate; the
        legislative-district map slices Philadelphia and Pittsburgh further
        using parcel coordinates.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Population change 2020 → 2024</strong> &mdash; Census Population
        Estimates Program (PEP) sub-county vintage 2024 estimates compared
        against 2020 Decennial counts. Where the PEP estimate is missing
        (very small munis can be suppressed) the muni shows &ldquo;&mdash;.&rdquo;
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Median home value</strong> and <strong>rent-burdened %</strong>
        &mdash; ACS 2023 5-year estimates (B25077 and B25070
        respectively). For the per-municipality choropleth above we
        use the muni-level ACS pull; for the per-district snapshot
        panel we re-aggregate the same tables at the census-tract
        level (~3,400 PA tracts, ~380 of them in Philadelphia) so
        each district inside a big city gets its own number rather
        than the city-wide average. ACS suppresses estimates for very
        small populations, which surface as &ldquo;&mdash;.&rdquo;
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Per-district popchange limitation</strong> &mdash;
        Census PEP only publishes population estimates down to the
        municipality. For districts entirely inside a single muni
        (e.g. every Philadelphia House district), the snapshot
        popchange is the muni-wide figure. We don&rsquo;t synthesize
        sub-muni popchange because the source data simply isn&rsquo;t
        published at that level.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why a separate map?</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/">main map</a> is built for one job: showing where
      legislators stand on housing bills. Stuffing muni-level housing
      stats into the same view crowded the screen with two different
      stories. This page is for the second story &mdash; where in the
      Commonwealth is housing growing, stagnating, or shrinking, and
      where the affordability pressure is highest &mdash; on its own
      terms, with controls tuned for that question.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
